\subsection{Definitions and mathematical statement}
\paragraph{Statement recorded in the supplied source.}
Let $f:X\to Y$ be a connected-fiber surjective morphism of smooth connected projective complex varieties. Let E and D be reduced SNC divisors on X and Y with $Supp(f*D)\subset Supp(E)$. Assume f and every boundary stratum of E are smooth over Y minus Supp(D). For a very general fiber F with $E_F=E|F$, must $\kappa (X,K_X+E)=\kappa (Y,K_Y+D)+\kappa (F,K_F+E_F)$, with $-\infty $ absorbing in the sum?

\subsection{Short English statement}
Let $f:X\to Y$ be a connected-fiber surjective morphism of smooth connected projective complex varieties. Let E and D be reduced SNC divisors on X and Y with $Supp(f*D)\subset Supp(E)$. Assume f and every boundary stratum of E are smooth over Y minus Supp(D). For a very general fiber F with $E_F=E|F$, must $\kappa (X,K_X+E)=\kappa (Y,K_Y+D)+\kappa (F,K_F+E_F)$, with $-\infty $ absorbing in the sum?

\subsection{Sources}
\begin{itemize}
\item[S1] Original question source or mathematical context.
\url{https://arxiv.org/abs/0806.4413}.
\item[S2] OpenAI, manuscript citation page. Source of the formulation motivating this local problem.
\url{https://github.com/openai/math/blob/adc7f1241b42e322a6451854ab7e4b4c146bf78a/preprints/The-reverse-logarithmic-Kodaira-inequality-and-additivity-September-26-2026/README.md}.
\end{itemize}

