\section*{Logarithmic Kodaira additivity under stratum smoothness}

\subsection{OpenAI's claim}
For reduced normal-crossing projective pairs that are smooth on every boundary stratum over the open base, OpenAI claims logarithmic Kodaira additivity, including the cases with negative-infinite dimension. The cited manuscript argues for that result; its relation to this problem is specified below.

\subsection{Outline of the argument}
Manuscript 1 (family 033, 2026-09-26): Iterated logarithmic cotangent and Higgs maps turn a selected pluriform into base sections with controlled coefficients. Period-image positivity and a canonical bundle formula supply the reverse inequality; combining it with subadditivity yields equality under the stated stratum-smoothness assumptions.

\subsection{Scope relative to this problem}
Manuscript 1 (family 033, 2026-09-26): The problem selects the mathematical statement summarized here, including its stated hypotheses and parameter range. The cited theorem gives a claimed proof of that assertion in full.

\subsection{Formal verification}
For family 033, the scope page documents the following selections. Only the case of a negative-infinite logarithmic fiber dimension is selected; it forces negative-infinite dimension upstairs and vanishing of positive pluriforms. Finite-dimensional fibers and the negative-base case remain outside the linked statement. This entry does not assert that the selected declarations formalize every part of manuscript 1's main conclusion. Comparator files: LogKodairaFiberNegative.lean. Declarations: OAI.ReverseLogKodaira.SmoothProjectiveVariety.veryGenerally\_fiber\_negative\_branch.

This site has not executed the Lean files or checked their dependencies. These descriptions report the scope of the linked declarations, rather than a new verification of the proofs.

\subsection{Status caveat}
OpenAI attributes these results to an internal model. Its release notice cautions that results lacking full formalization can contain mistakes. This site has not independently assessed the proofs; a solved label records the scope of the reported claim.

